173
00:13:45,869 --> 00:13:52,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, regardless of the window, you realize that, OK, if I can see, smell, taste,

174
00:13:53,187 --> 00:14:00,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Perceive these five senses without my mind moving, flipping over, perturbing, twisting, running towards.

175
00:14:02,609 --> 00:14:03,309
[SPEAKER_00]: I can sustain it.

176
00:14:04,389 --> 00:14:13,991
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is a pressure, a challenge, a provoke to give in and start tumbling down, basically, you know, through that momentum of I gave in and now I can't stop.

177
00:14:14,611 --> 00:14:16,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that's the danger.

178
00:14:16,851 --> 00:14:17,631
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't do that.

179
00:14:18,631 --> 00:14:21,652
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when something comes to provoke, to hook you, to bait you,

180
00:14:22,370 --> 00:14:23,011
[SPEAKER_00]: That's okay.

181
00:14:23,031 --> 00:14:23,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Nope.

182
00:14:24,434 --> 00:14:33,068
[SPEAKER_00]: I refuse to look until it disappears, because it could result in this mind getting up and running and tripping over and falling out of the window and meeting death or deadly suffering.

183
00:14:34,530 --> 00:14:43,852
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean, oh, because I get hooked to these particular things that I see, I must block and board and destroy all these windows and never see anything again.

184
00:14:44,392 --> 00:14:46,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you are still.

185
00:14:46,213 --> 00:14:48,833
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to get off the chair to block those windows.

186
00:14:48,853 --> 00:14:52,074
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to get off the chair to chase things away that are in front of the windows.

187
00:14:52,114 --> 00:14:54,175
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize, that was the problem.

188
00:14:54,295 --> 00:14:56,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Not what was in front of the window that I could see.

189
00:14:57,375 --> 00:14:59,356
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem was that I was getting up on account of it.

190
00:14:59,756 --> 00:15:01,936
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem was that I wanted it, so I got up.

191
00:15:02,357 --> 00:15:04,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I didn't want it, so I got up.

192
00:15:04,937 --> 00:15:26,404
[SPEAKER_00]: When the mind is completely, completely, completely cooled down, there is no basis of passion, even memories of passion, to provoke mind to get up from that chair, and you encounter things that used to provoke lust or aversion, and now you realize that moving on account of it is inconceivable, because the mind has been so tamed, and that's the... and the Buddha said that like, oh, yeah,

193
00:15:26,984 --> 00:15:36,630
[SPEAKER_00]: When Ananda asked him, so there's a mind that's enlightened, do they always live in the pleasure of enlightenment, or do they like calm down and kind of go back to normal, so to speak?

194
00:15:37,030 --> 00:15:47,917
[SPEAKER_00]: No, the pleasure calms down, but then when that mind would encounter things that used to provoke lust, aversion, distraction, he would re-experience that pleasure of freedom, because it would remind him of the freedom.

195
00:15:48,828 --> 00:15:59,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you would, obviously, you would know the significance, the peril, the danger of each time, even in the past, you got off that chair and try to get, you know, what provoked you or get rid of what provoked you.

196
00:15:59,953 --> 00:16:04,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, fully understanding the danger will then enable you to not give in and get off the chair.

197
00:16:04,715 --> 00:16:06,356
[SPEAKER_00]: And it will then eventually calm you down.

198
00:16:06,896 --> 00:16:11,198
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, because you haven't been feeding the passion, there will be no more passion pressuring you.

199
00:16:11,635 --> 00:16:23,622
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you have these things, they used to provoke you, and you're absolutely motionless, like, there is absolutely no movement in the mind on account of it, with the memory of how much you used to be controlled by that.

200
00:16:24,723 --> 00:16:27,765
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like, oh, wow, you look at the mind, it is truly free.

201
00:16:28,524 --> 00:16:31,085
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when the monk would recognize that.

202
00:16:31,605 --> 00:16:43,529
[SPEAKER_01]: And if there's something that's not provoking you, that's something that is calm, let's say, you realize that, oh, I can just eat plain porridge.

203
00:16:44,450 --> 00:16:46,490
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't make me more hungry.

204
00:16:47,090 --> 00:16:47,971
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't crave it.

205
00:16:49,492 --> 00:16:50,553
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't hate it either.

206
00:16:50,573 --> 00:16:51,053
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't hate it.

207
00:16:51,073 --> 00:16:51,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

208
00:16:51,473 --> 00:16:52,494
[SPEAKER_01]: I can sustain myself with that.

209
00:16:52,554 --> 00:16:53,895
[SPEAKER_01]: If it goes, there's no problem.

210
00:16:53,935 --> 00:16:55,457
[SPEAKER_01]: But isn't that wonderful?

211
00:16:56,297 --> 00:16:56,798
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's wonderful.

212
00:16:56,818 --> 00:16:57,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't that wonderful?

213
00:16:57,679 --> 00:17:00,281
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's wonderful because it's extremely practical.

214
00:17:00,761 --> 00:17:00,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

215
00:17:01,061 --> 00:17:02,743
[SPEAKER_00]: And practicality is very wonderful, very nice.

216
00:17:02,763 --> 00:17:03,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So you see the joy in it.

217
00:17:03,784 --> 00:17:04,905
[SPEAKER_00]: But this thing is not provocative.

218
00:17:05,065 --> 00:17:05,605
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not provocative.

219
00:17:05,625 --> 00:17:08,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Isn't it wonderful to just wear, you know, patch ropes?

220
00:17:09,388 --> 00:17:10,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't worry about it.

221
00:17:10,629 --> 00:17:11,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Simple clothes.

222
00:17:11,470 --> 00:17:12,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Very simple.

223
00:17:13,112 --> 00:17:15,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Serves the purpose.

224
00:17:15,253 --> 00:17:16,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Lightweight, easy to patch.

225
00:17:17,295 --> 00:17:20,738
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't have to do anything in order to, you know, procure it, get it.

226
00:17:21,058 --> 00:17:21,819
[SPEAKER_00]: And nobody wants it.

227
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:23,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody else wants it.

228
00:17:23,941 --> 00:17:25,242
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just like, oh, that's wonderful.

229
00:17:25,502 --> 00:17:28,225
[SPEAKER_00]: It's absolutely burdenless, absolutely easy.

230
00:17:28,946 --> 00:17:30,087
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's pleasant.

231
00:17:30,427 --> 00:17:31,628
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not sensuality.

232
00:17:33,226 --> 00:17:34,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Generosity as well.

233
00:17:34,468 --> 00:17:42,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's why the Buddha would, to the lay people, before he would teach them about the view, he would, like, elevate, gladden their mind with talks on generosity and rebirth and stuff.

234
00:17:42,959 --> 00:17:44,181
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, good pleasure.

235
00:17:45,022 --> 00:17:46,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Generosity is a good pleasure.

236
00:17:46,364 --> 00:17:47,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Helping others is a good...

237
00:17:48,315 --> 00:18:02,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, even if you're not doing it for the purest of the reasons, still, helping others, being generous, giving, it's actually a good type of pleasure that is beneficial for the mind, because it's not the pleasure that will provoke lust, thirst.

238
00:18:02,743 --> 00:18:08,686
[SPEAKER_00]: People's lives are controlled by thirst, by fever of their mind, rooted in lust.

239
00:18:09,210 --> 00:18:11,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Rooted in aversion, rooted in distraction.

240
00:18:11,412 --> 00:18:17,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Just on account of, yes, those few particular things that that provoke pleasure.

241
00:18:17,516 --> 00:18:19,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the entire I-domain and sights.

242
00:18:21,298 --> 00:18:23,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Just particular sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

243
00:18:23,560 --> 00:18:25,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, that is the most important.

244
00:18:25,201 --> 00:18:31,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, that becomes the criteria for a mind that's dependent on that sensual being.

245
00:18:31,385 --> 00:18:32,746
[SPEAKER_00]: That becomes criteria for everything else.

246
00:18:34,010 --> 00:18:47,022
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if they could see the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of generosity, until they see the danger of the other type of pleasure, that particular type of sensual pleasure, that is THE pleasure that controls the mind, everything else would be secondary to that pleasure.

247
00:18:48,303 --> 00:18:53,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, it's a very kind of... it's a narrow bait, so to speak.

248
00:18:53,808 --> 00:18:55,470
[SPEAKER_00]: There is more of a non-bait around.

249
00:18:55,570 --> 00:18:58,973
[SPEAKER_00]: There are more things that are not beautiful in the world, than things that are beautiful.

250
00:19:00,548 --> 00:19:03,049
[SPEAKER_00]: There are more ugly things than pretty things, generally.

251
00:19:03,309 --> 00:19:04,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-provocative.

252
00:19:04,510 --> 00:19:06,671
[SPEAKER_00]: There are exactly more things that are non-provocative.

253
00:19:07,411 --> 00:19:16,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you haven't understood that, that the gratification, the danger and the escape in regard to the provocative things, that is your entire being.

254
00:19:16,535 --> 00:19:18,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Kama, bhava, is the entire state of existence.

255
00:19:19,697 --> 00:19:24,239
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of those few baits, few little hooks that you're constantly attached to.

256
00:19:25,023 --> 00:19:26,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And they drag you left and right.

257
00:19:26,944 --> 00:19:34,847
[SPEAKER_00]: And you see there is more of this, but because you're not developed in regard to these particular provocative pleasures, you are in their control.

258
00:19:35,027 --> 00:19:38,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can't do anything about it until you fully understand and free yourself from that.

259
00:19:40,169 --> 00:19:49,873
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, the entire being needs to be uprooted, because that entire being depends on getting off the chair to look at that which is very pressuringly beautiful.

260
00:19:50,838 --> 00:19:55,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting off the chair to, you know, go and shout at that, which is very pressuringly averse.

261
00:19:56,168 --> 00:20:01,171
[SPEAKER_01]: But you only do that, you only get up, because you don't see that as a danger.

262
00:20:01,391 --> 00:20:05,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you don't see getting up as a danger.

263
00:20:05,092 --> 00:20:10,075
[SPEAKER_00]: You're fully immersed with that particular chord of sensual pleasure.

264
00:20:10,115 --> 00:20:14,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can force myself to sit down.

265
00:20:14,277 --> 00:20:15,037
[SPEAKER_01]: I mustn't get up.

266
00:20:15,218 --> 00:20:17,539
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's probably how you have to start.

267
00:20:17,579 --> 00:20:19,720
[SPEAKER_00]: You took it on faith that you shouldn't get up.

268
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:20,880
[SPEAKER_01]: But I want to get up.

269
00:20:21,401 --> 00:20:22,101
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to get up.

270
00:20:22,181 --> 00:20:23,142
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not getting up.

271
00:20:23,502 --> 00:20:24,422
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the first step.

272
00:20:24,682 --> 00:20:25,763
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the sense restraint.

273
00:20:27,555 --> 00:20:30,798
[SPEAKER_00]: And because you're not getting up, means you are not acting out of it.

274
00:20:31,118 --> 00:20:33,060
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not sustaining the habit of getting up.

275
00:20:33,940 --> 00:20:41,446
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sooner or later, if you keep doing that, you're going to start seeing more in a sense of, ah, I haven't been getting up, factually.

276
00:20:41,967 --> 00:20:43,348
[SPEAKER_00]: The aggregative virtue is there.

277
00:20:43,748 --> 00:20:45,330
[SPEAKER_00]: I haven't been breaking the precepts.

278
00:20:46,282 --> 00:20:50,265
[SPEAKER_00]: I was very pressured to do so and everything, but I haven't been doing it.

279
00:20:50,685 --> 00:20:52,206
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is already increasing perspective.

280
00:20:52,266 --> 00:20:52,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Already, yeah.

281
00:20:53,047 --> 00:20:53,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

282
00:20:53,988 --> 00:20:56,690
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you're already closer to see, like, right.

283
00:20:57,170 --> 00:20:58,811
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that was the problem.

284
00:20:58,931 --> 00:20:59,652
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the trick.

285
00:20:59,812 --> 00:21:00,553
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the bait.

286
00:21:01,053 --> 00:21:01,854
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the bait.

287
00:21:03,155 --> 00:21:07,958
[SPEAKER_00]: That was just to hook you, but the bait can't come through the window and hook you out.

288
00:21:08,499 --> 00:21:10,020
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one who goes for it.

289
00:21:11,641 --> 00:21:12,541
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, what's the danger?

290
00:21:12,582 --> 00:21:14,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the danger that there are hooks around?

291
00:21:14,939 --> 00:21:17,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is the danger in the fact that you can't stop grabbing them?

292
00:21:20,303 --> 00:21:23,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but if only there were no hooks, I wouldn't be grabbing them.

293
00:21:23,405 --> 00:21:26,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, now you're even avoiding responsibility for grabbing the hooks.

294
00:21:27,908 --> 00:21:30,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, if there were only no māra and saṃsāra, it would be great.

295
00:21:30,811 --> 00:21:34,133
[SPEAKER_00]: But there are... there is... there are hooks, there is saṃsāra, there is sensuality.

296
00:21:34,673 --> 00:21:37,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the other thing that you could equally do that would free you from it?

297
00:21:37,635 --> 00:21:39,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, stop reaching for the hooks.

298
00:21:41,258 --> 00:21:50,744
[SPEAKER_00]: All right, so I have to take responsibility first for when I do reach for the hooks, and not blame the hooks for my reaching, and then calm down, and then stop reaching, and then see the freedom for myself.

299
00:21:52,985 --> 00:22:00,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, can I... can I stop reach... can I understand the danger while... while reaching?

300
00:22:00,429 --> 00:22:01,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

301
00:22:01,870 --> 00:22:10,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I understand the danger of reaching for a hook, while at the same time are unable to resist and stop reaching from a hook?

302
00:22:11,461 --> 00:22:15,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Means the fact that you are reaching for a hook means you're not seeing the danger.

303
00:22:17,443 --> 00:22:18,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the danger.

304
00:22:18,743 --> 00:22:19,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting up.

305
00:22:21,564 --> 00:22:27,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting yourself there, where then you will be... you will be in control of it, not the other way around.

306
00:22:28,947 --> 00:22:35,349
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you think, okay, so if I can remain on the chair and look at the things that are provocative of lust and thirst, that's impossible.

307
00:22:36,717 --> 00:22:48,007
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's impossible to have perceptions of things that are sensual pleasures, without perverting that order and putting that first eye, without getting out of the chair.

308
00:22:51,290 --> 00:23:02,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the fact is, again, you might not be getting out of the chair, but the view, the sights, the perceptions are pressuring you, and you don't see where the problem is, because you're still looking.

309
00:23:06,115 --> 00:23:08,676
[SPEAKER_00]: If you realize where the problem is, you will automatically stop looking.

310
00:23:09,756 --> 00:23:10,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, right.

311
00:23:10,476 --> 00:23:13,597
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is in the mind that wants to go there.

312
00:23:13,797 --> 00:23:23,499
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would I be provoking this rabid dog that can harm me, that I'm paired with, instead of calming it?

313
00:23:23,819 --> 00:23:29,100
[SPEAKER_00]: There is something provocative that can result in, you know, ripping my arm off when it charges for it.

314
00:23:30,080 --> 00:23:31,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would I even do that?

315
00:23:31,682 --> 00:23:37,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless I don't see the danger of a provoked dog, which is what my mind is.

316
00:23:37,408 --> 00:23:40,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's when people think they can engage with centrality mindfully.

317
00:23:40,572 --> 00:23:41,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

318
00:23:42,294 --> 00:23:44,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Means there's already no perspective, if you choose to engage.

319
00:23:45,117 --> 00:23:47,379
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's to that... this... oh, I'll just let it look.

320
00:23:48,327 --> 00:23:52,550
[SPEAKER_00]: I won't engage physically or verbally, but mentally I'll think about... That's what I said.

321
00:23:53,050 --> 00:24:02,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Physically withdrawn, speech purified, and mentally he does not engage with, he doesn't accept, welcome, entertain thoughts of sensuality.

322
00:24:03,136 --> 00:24:13,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that would be the equivalent of being strapped to a rabid mad animal that is calm, and then you show it things that are making it even more rabid and mad.

323
00:24:14,395 --> 00:24:15,276
[SPEAKER_00]: While you're strapped to it.

324
00:24:16,056 --> 00:24:23,883
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, I can hold it for now, but the point will come when my grip will loosen or something, and then that animal will not remember me.

325
00:24:25,123 --> 00:24:26,124
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna just go wild.

326
00:24:26,264 --> 00:24:29,387
[SPEAKER_00]: People don't realize that their mind has the mind of its own.

327
00:24:30,248 --> 00:24:31,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your mind has a mind of its own.

328
00:24:33,070 --> 00:24:34,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter your sense of ownership.

329
00:24:34,791 --> 00:24:35,652
[SPEAKER_00]: My mind, my mind.

330
00:24:35,812 --> 00:24:39,895
[SPEAKER_00]: The truth is, that mind, it's basically on its own.

331
00:24:40,736 --> 00:24:41,737
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm subjected to it.

332
00:24:42,523 --> 00:24:49,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the recognition of seeing the sign of the mind as something that is not yours, that you are paired with.

333
00:24:49,187 --> 00:24:50,708
[SPEAKER_00]: As citta nimitta we often mention.

334
00:24:51,789 --> 00:24:54,230
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like being able to see the mind on its own.

335
00:24:54,991 --> 00:25:00,975
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, okay, so, it's not really like, no, but I should be able to look at this without being pressured.

336
00:25:01,055 --> 00:25:01,795
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not up to you.

337
00:25:02,175 --> 00:25:07,158
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind has a mind of its own, that if sees these things, it will go for it.

338
00:25:07,239 --> 00:25:08,860
[SPEAKER_00]: If it doesn't see it, it will calm down.

339
00:25:09,420 --> 00:25:10,841
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's now your wisdom.

340
00:25:11,783 --> 00:25:14,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so I won't let it look through that window until things clear out there.

341
00:25:15,206 --> 00:25:16,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, look, now it's on pleasant sides.

342
00:25:16,568 --> 00:25:17,348
[SPEAKER_00]: They will calm me down.

343
00:25:17,408 --> 00:25:17,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, look.

344
00:25:19,030 --> 00:25:23,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not then feeling like completely, you know, suffocated.

345
00:25:23,054 --> 00:25:24,755
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't look at anything.

346
00:25:24,795 --> 00:25:25,035
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

347
00:25:25,055 --> 00:25:32,542
[SPEAKER_00]: You can look at things that are not provocative of lust, aversion, distraction, indolence, pressure to break the precepts, all of that.

348
00:25:32,662 --> 00:25:34,624
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know that.

349
00:25:34,684 --> 00:25:36,266
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very obvious what those things are.

350
00:25:38,872 --> 00:25:46,835
[SPEAKER_01]: But it takes that faith, just that first attempt of just, okay, let me give it a go for a while, that restraint.

351
00:25:46,975 --> 00:25:48,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, of course.

352
00:25:48,416 --> 00:25:51,517
[SPEAKER_00]: None of this would make sense if a person doesn't want to do it.

353
00:25:51,577 --> 00:25:57,079
[SPEAKER_00]: If a person just is content with the view that everything is sensuality or nothing is sensuality.

354
00:25:57,299 --> 00:26:01,381
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you can do whatever you want or you shouldn't be doing anything, because everything is bad.

355
00:26:01,461 --> 00:26:02,601
